RG.deep["union-sonship"] = {
  sections: [
    {
      title: "Word study: ‘placing as a son’, now and not yet",
      body: [
        "The Greek <em>huiothesia</em> joins <em>huios</em> (son) with a form of <em>tithēmi</em> (to place or set). Bible Hub’s lexicons give its plain sense as ‘the placing as a son’. It is a legal and family word at once: a new name, a new household, a new inheritance.",
        "Thayer’s lexicon notices that Paul uses it in two ways. In Romans 9:4 the adoption belongs to Israel, God’s ‘firstborn son’ (Exodus 4:22). In Romans 8:15, Galatians 4:5 and Ephesians 1:5 it describes all who share Christ’s life by the Spirit. The story runs from one nation to all nations, not from one nation to none.",
        "There is a surprise in Romans 8:23. Paul says we who already have the Spirit are still ‘waiting for adoption, the redemption of our body’. So sonship is real now, but it is not finished until our bodies are raised. We are sons and daughters already, and we are also on the way to becoming visibly what we are (1 John 3:2)."
      ],
      refs: ["Rom 8:15", "Rom 8:23", "Rom 9:4", "Gal 4:5", "Eph 1:5", "Exod 4:22", "1 John 3:2"]
    },
    {
      title: "‘Abba, Father’: Jesus’ own prayer given to us",
      body: [
        "<em>Abba</em> appears only three times in the New Testament: Mark 14:36, Romans 8:15 and Galatians 4:6. Each time the Aramaic word is followed by the Greek <em>ho patēr</em>, ‘the Father’. Thayer suggests the pair became a fixed form of prayer. It may also be a picture of the early church itself: Aramaic-speaking and Greek-speaking believers calling on one Father together.",
        "Notice where Jesus says it. Not on a mountaintop, but in Gethsemane, in agony, praying ‘not what I desire, but what you desire’ (Mark 14:36). So when the Spirit puts ‘Abba’ in our mouths, he gives us the Son’s trust in the darkest place, not only in the bright ones. That is why Paul links sonship with suffering: we are heirs ‘if indeed we suffer with him’ (Romans 8:17).",
        "The Spirit does the crying. In Galatians 4:6 the Spirit cries ‘Abba’ in our hearts; in Romans 8:15 we cry it by the Spirit. Prayer, at its root, is not us reaching a distant God. It is the Son’s own conversation with the Father, overheard and shared."
      ],
      refs: ["Mark 14:36", "Rom 8:15–17", "Gal 4:6", "Heb 5:7–8"]
    },
    {
      title: "Close reading: Galatians 3:23–4:7",
      body: [
        "Paul tells a family story. An heir who is still a child ‘is no different from a bondservant, though he is lord of all’ (4:1). He is under guardians and stewards ‘until the day appointed by the father’ (4:2). The point: the child was always the heir. What changes is not who owns the estate but when the son comes into it.",
        "The law is pictured as a <em>paidagōgos</em> (3:24), the household slave who walked a boy to school. Then ‘in the fullness of time’ God sent his Son (4:4). The Son goes all the way into our condition, ‘born of a woman, born under the law’, so that we might receive adoption.",
        "Everything happens ‘in Christ Jesus’ (3:26). We ‘put on Christ’ (3:27), and in him there is neither Jew nor Greek, slave nor free, male and female (3:28). We are not sons alongside the Son. We are sons <em>in</em> the Son, and his relationship with the Father becomes the space we live in."
      ],
      refs: ["Gal 3:23–29", "Gal 4:1–7", "John 17:23", "Rom 8:29"]
    },
    {
      title: "How the early church read sonship",
      body: [
        "The church fathers loved a pattern often called ‘the great exchange’. Irenaeus (around AD 180) wrote that the Son of God became the Son of man so that man, taken into the Word and receiving adoption, might become a son of God. He also asked how we could share the adoption of sons unless the Word, made flesh, had entered into communion with us.",
        "Athanasius said the same thing more boldly: ‘He was made man that we might be made God’. He did not mean we become extra gods. He meant we share by grace in the life the Son has by nature. The Son is Son by nature; we are sons and daughters by adoption and grace, but it is truly his life we share.",
        "Later, John Calvin, a Reformer not usually linked with this stream, put union at the centre: as long as Christ stays outside us, nothing he did benefits us; he must become ours and dwell in us. On that point the fathers, the Reformers and the relational gospel agree. Sonship is not a reward next to Christ. It is Christ himself, shared."
      ],
      refs: ["John 1:12–14", "2 Pet 1:4", "Gal 2:20", "Col 1:27"]
    },
    {
      title: "Two pictures, born and adopted, and the strongest counter-reading",
      body: [
        "John and Paul use different family pictures. John speaks of <em>tekna</em>, children ‘born of God’ (John 1:12–13; 1 John 3:9). Paul speaks of <em>huiothesia</em>, adoption. Birth stresses new life from within. Adoption stresses a new legal place and name. The New Testament needs both: God gives us a new nature and a new standing.",
        "The strongest traditional reading says: adoption happens when a person believes. Classic Reformed confessions, such as the Westminster Confession (chapter 12, ‘Of Adoption’), describe it as a grace given to those who are justified by faith. John 1:12 seems to say the same: to those who received him, he gave the right to become children.",
        "The relational reply is not to deny faith but to ask what faith receives. Teachers in the line of T. F. Torrance and C. Baxter Kruger argue that Christ’s own sonship, lived in our flesh, is the ground of ours, and that faith is the Spirit’s gift by which we come to share and know it. Both sides agree that sonship comes only in Christ and is received through the Spirit. They differ on whether humanity as a whole is already included in that sonship before it is known. That difference is real and should be stated honestly."
      ],
      refs: ["John 1:12–13", "1 John 3:1–2", "Eph 1:4–5", "2 Cor 5:14"]
    },
    {
      title: "Practical meaning: living as heirs",
      body: [
        "Pray as a child. You do not need special words to get God’s attention. ‘Abba’ is enough, even when you can only say it in tears.",
        "Let fear lose its job. Paul contrasts the ‘spirit of bondage again to fear’ with the Spirit of adoption (Romans 8:15). When you notice you are trying to earn your place, stop and remember whose house you live in.",
        "Read hardship differently. Hebrews 12:5–11 calls God’s training the treatment of sons, not punishment of strangers. And let your sonship turn outward: ‘pure religion’ is visiting ‘the fatherless and widows in their affliction’ (James 1:27). Those who have been adopted make room for others."
      ],
      refs: ["Rom 8:15", "Heb 12:5–11", "Jas 1:27", "Eph 5:1", "Matt 6:9"]
    }
  ],
  evidence: [
    { text: "Huiothesia: ‘the placing as a son’; Topical Lexicon: ‘The Greek term υἱοθεσία (Strong’s 5206) literally means “placing as a son.”’ It occurs five times: Romans 8:15, 8:23, 9:4; Galatians 4:5; Ephesians 1:5.", source: "Bible Hub, Strong’s Greek 5206", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/5206.htm" },
    { text: "Thayer on Romans 8:23: ‘to wait for adoption, i. e. the consummate condition of the sons of God, which will render it evident that they are the sons of God’.", source: "Thayer’s Greek Lexicon, via Bible Hub 5206", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/5206.htm" },
    { text: "Abba is ‘a customary title of God in prayer. Whenever it occurs in the N. T. (Mark 14:36; Romans 8:15; Galatians 4:6) it has the Greek interpretation subjoined to it’.", source: "Thayer’s Greek Lexicon, via Bible Hub 5", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/5.htm" },
    { text: "‘For it was for this end that the Word of God was made man, and He who was the Son of God became the Son of man, that man, having been taken into the Word, and receiving the adoption, might become the son of God.’", source: "Irenaeus, Against Heresies III.19.1", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0103319.htm" },
    { text: "‘For, in what way could we be partaken of the adoption of sons, unless we had received from Him through the Son that fellowship which refers to Himself, unless His Word, having been made flesh, had entered into communion with us?’", source: "Irenaeus, Against Heresies III.18.7", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0103318.htm" },
    { text: "‘For He was made man that we might be made God; and He manifested Himself by a body that we might receive the idea of the unseen Father’.", source: "Athanasius, On the Incarnation 54", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/2802.htm" },
    { text: "‘So long as we are without Christ and separated from him, nothing which he suffered and did for the salvation of the human race is of the least benefit to us. To communicate to us the blessings which he received from the Father, he must become ours and dwell in us.’", source: "John Calvin, Institutes III.1.1", url: "https://ccel.org/ccel/calvin/institutes.v.ii.html" }
  ]
};

