RG.deep["designed-for-connection"] = {
  sections: [
    {
      title: "Word study: ‘a helper comparable to him’",
      body: [
        "Genesis 2:18 calls the woman an <em>ʿezer kenegdo</em>. The KJV rendered it ‘an help meet for him’, which later turned into the English word ‘helpmeet’, as if it meant a lesser assistant. The Hebrew says something else.",
        "<em>ʿEzer</em> means ‘help’ or ‘succour’. It appears 21 times in the Hebrew Bible, and most of them describe God himself: ‘My help comes from Yahweh, who made heaven and earth’ (Psalm 121:2); ‘he is our help and our shield’ (Psalm 33:20). An <em>ʿezer</em> is not a servant. It is strength that comes to meet a real need.",
        "<em>Kenegdo</em> comes from <em>neged</em>, ‘in front of’ or ‘opposite’. The idea is someone who corresponds to you, who can stand face to face with you. The first human need in the Bible is not a task to be done but a person to be known by."
      ],
      refs: ["Gen 2:18–25", "Ps 121:1–2", "Ps 33:20", "Deut 33:26–29", "Exod 18:4"]
    },
    {
      title: "Close reading: the ‘not good’ in a very good world",
      body: [
        "Genesis 1 repeats ‘it was good’ and ends with ‘very good’ (1:31). Then Genesis 2:18 says ‘it is not good for the man to be alone’. Nothing has gone wrong yet. There is no sin in the garden. The only thing missing is communion.",
        "God then brings the animals to the man to name (2:19–20). Naming is real work and real fellowship with creation, but ‘there was not found a helper comparable to him’. Even a perfect job in a perfect world does not meet the deepest need.",
        "The scene ends with a picture of complete openness: ‘The man and his wife were both naked, and they were not ashamed’ (2:25). That is what connection looks like before fear: to be fully seen and not afraid. Genesis 3 will show the opposite, and the rest of the Bible tells how God brings us back."
      ],
      refs: ["Gen 1:31", "Gen 2:18–20", "Gen 2:24–25", "Gen 3:7–10"]
    },
    {
      title: "The God who is love has always been relationship",
      body: [
        "‘God is love’ (1 John 4:8, 16). Love needs someone to love. Christians say this is true of God before creation: the Father loved the Son ‘before the foundation of the world’ (John 17:24). God did not make us because he was lonely. He made us because his love was overflowing.",
        "Augustine, in <em>On the Trinity</em> Book VIII, noticed that love always involves three things: one who loves, one who is loved, and the love itself. He offered this as a pointer toward the Trinity, and he himself said such pictures fall short. Still, it shows why a God who is love is a God of relationship.",
        "Augustine’s most famous line says the same from the human side: ‘You have made us for Yourself, and our hearts are restless until they rest in You.’ Our hunger for connection is not a weakness to be outgrown. It is the shape of our creation."
      ],
      refs: ["1 John 4:8, 16", "John 17:24", "John 17:21–23", "Ps 42:1–2"]
    },
    {
      title: "Connection broken: the anatomy of hiding",
      body: [
        "Genesis 3 shows broken trust spreading in every direction. Toward God: ‘I was afraid … and I hid myself’ (3:10). Toward each other: ‘The woman whom you gave to be with me, she gave me’ (3:12). Toward creation: the ground is cursed and work becomes toil (3:17–19). In the next chapter it reaches family: Cain kills Abel and asks, ‘Am I my brother’s keeper?’ (4:9).",
        "But notice God’s first words after the Fall. Not a verdict, but a question: ‘Where are you?’ (3:9). God comes looking. He clothes the ashamed couple (3:21). The rest of Scripture is that search continued, through Abraham, in whom ‘all the families of the earth will be blessed’ (12:3).",
        "In Christ the broken lines are restored together. He ‘broke down the middle wall of separation’ to make ‘one new man’ (Ephesians 2:14–16). John writes so that ‘you also may have fellowship (<em>koinōnia</em>) with us. Yes, and our fellowship is with the Father, and with his Son, Jesus Christ’ (1 John 1:3). Fellowship with God and with each other come as one gift."
      ],
      refs: ["Gen 3:8–12", "Gen 3:21", "Gen 4:9", "Gen 12:3", "Eph 2:14–16", "1 John 1:3"]
    },
    {
      title: "Echoes in research, and their limits",
      body: [
        "The Harvard Study of Adult Development has followed people since 1938. Reporting on it in 2017, the Harvard Gazette summarised the finding plainly: close relationships, more than money or fame, keep people happy across their lives. The study’s director, the psychiatrist Robert Waldinger, said that loneliness is as harmful as smoking or alcoholism.",
        "This should be used with care. The study began with a small group of American men, and research like this shows strong links, not simple causes. Faith does not rest on it.",
        "But it is a striking echo of Genesis 2:18. What Scripture said at the beginning, careful research keeps finding: human beings do not flourish alone."
      ],
      refs: ["Gen 2:18", "Eccl 4:9–12", "Prov 18:24"]
    },
    {
      title: "Counter-reading: is the image of God relational, royal or rational?",
      body: [
        "Christians have read ‘the image of God’ in three main ways. Many church fathers and medieval writers stressed reason and the soul. Many Old Testament scholars today read it as a royal role: ancient kings set up images of themselves to mark their rule, and Genesis 1:26–28 joins the image to ‘dominion’. Karl Barth and others stressed relationship, ‘male and female’.",
        "Some theologians also warn against building too much on the Trinity as a model for human community. Karen Kilby, for example, has argued that ‘social’ pictures of the Trinity can simply project our own ideals onto God. This is a fair caution.",
        "We do not have to choose only one reading. The royal reading is itself relational: humans are to care for the world together, as God’s representatives, ‘male and female’. And the New Testament says the true image is Christ (Colossians 1:15; 2 Corinthians 4:4), who lived wholly in communion with the Father and wholly for others. To be made in his image is to be made for that kind of life."
      ],
      refs: ["Gen 1:26–28", "Gen 9:6", "Col 1:15", "2 Cor 4:4", "Ps 8:4–6"]
    },
    {
      title: "Practical meaning: becoming known",
      body: [
        "Healing in Scripture often happens between people, not only between a person and God. ‘Confess your offences to one another, and pray for one another, that you may be healed’ (James 5:16). ‘Bear one another’s burdens’ (Galatians 6:2).",
        "So take small risks of being known. Share a meal (Romans 12:13 calls it ‘hospitality’). Tell one safe friend the truth about how you are. Choose one relationship to tend on purpose this week.",
        "Solitude still has its place. But in Scripture, time alone with God sends us back to people. Isolation is not holiness, and being known in love is part of being saved."
      ],
      refs: ["Jas 5:16", "Gal 6:2", "Rom 12:10–13", "Heb 10:24–25", "Luke 5:16"]
    }
  ],
  evidence: [
    { text: "ʿEzer: ‘help, succour’ (Brown-Driver-Briggs); 21 occurrences, including Genesis 2:18, 20, Psalm 33:20 (‘He is our help and our shield’) and Psalm 121:1–2. KJV at Genesis 2:18: ‘an help meet for him’.", source: "Bible Hub, Strong’s Hebrew 5828", url: "https://biblehub.com/hebrew/5828.htm" },
    { text: "‘You have made us for Yourself, and our hearts are restless until they rest in You.’", source: "Augustine, Confessions I.1", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/110101.htm" },
    { text: "‘But love is of some one that loves, and with love something is loved. Behold, then, there are three things: he that loves, and that which is loved, and love.’", source: "Augustine, On the Trinity VIII", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/130108.htm" },
    { text: "‘Close relationships, more than money or fame, are what keep people happy throughout their lives, the study revealed.’", source: "Harvard Gazette (Liz Mineo, 2017), on the Harvard Study of Adult Development", url: "https://news.harvard.edu/gazette/story/2017/04/over-nearly-80-years-harvard-study-has-been-showing-how-to-live-a-healthy-and-happy-life/" },
    { text: "Robert Waldinger: ‘Loneliness kills. It’s as powerful as smoking or alcoholism.’", source: "Harvard Gazette (2017)", url: "https://news.harvard.edu/gazette/story/2017/04/over-nearly-80-years-harvard-study-has-been-showing-how-to-live-a-healthy-and-happy-life/" },
    { text: "‘For we have given nothing to Him previously, nor does He desire anything from us, as if He stood in need of it; but we do stand in need of fellowship with Him.’", source: "Irenaeus, Against Heresies V.2.1", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0103502.htm" },
    { text: "Of Christ’s life among us: ‘He passed through every stage of life, restoring to all communion with God.’", source: "Irenaeus, Against Heresies III.18.7", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0103318.htm" }
  ]
};

