RG.deep["church-now"] = {
  sections: [
    {
      title: "Word study: ekklēsia and Israel’s assembly",
      body: [
        "Thayer’s lexicon defines <em>ekklēsia</em> as ‘a gathering of citizens called out from their homes into some public place; an assembly’. Acts 19:39 still uses it for a city’s lawful assembly.",
        "More important for the Bible, the Greek Old Testament (the Septuagint) uses <em>ekklēsia</em> for the Hebrew <em>qahal</em>, the assembly of Israel gathered before God (Deuteronomy 31:30; Joshua 8:35). Stephen calls Israel at Sinai ‘the assembly in the wilderness’ (Acts 7:38). So when Jesus says ‘I will build my <em>ekklēsia</em>’ (Matthew 16:18), he is gathering a renewed Israel around himself, not starting a club.",
        "Jesus uses the word only twice (Matthew 16:18; 18:17). Its home is the life of the risen Lord with his people. The English word ‘church’ comes by another route, from <em>kyriakos</em>, ‘belonging to the Lord’."
      ],
      refs: ["Matt 16:18", "Matt 18:17", "Acts 7:38", "Acts 19:39", "Deut 31:30", "Heb 2:12"]
    },
    {
      title: "Close reading: Ephesians 2:11–22",
      body: [
        "Paul begins with distance: Gentiles were ‘far off’, ‘without God in the world’ (2:12–13). Then comes the turn: ‘now in Christ Jesus you who once were far off are made near in the blood of Christ’.",
        "Christ ‘is our peace’ and has ‘broken down the middle wall of separation’ (2:14). He creates ‘one new man’ out of two (2:15) and reconciles both to God ‘in one body through the cross’ (2:16). The church is reconciliation made visible. Where former enemies share one table, the gospel can be seen.",
        "Paul piles up pictures: ‘fellow citizens’ (a city), ‘the household of God’ (a family), ‘a holy temple’ (a dwelling place for God by the Spirit, 2:19–22). Peter adds that we are ‘living stones … built up as a spiritual house’ (1 Peter 2:5). And Paul says the church even shows God’s wisdom to the powers of the universe (3:10). A small gathering of reconciled people is a sign to the whole cosmos."
      ],
      refs: ["Eph 2:11–22", "Eph 3:10", "1 Pet 2:4–5", "Gal 3:28"]
    },
    {
      title: "Koinōnia: the table that makes one body",
      body: [
        "The first believers devoted themselves to the apostles’ teaching, to <em>koinōnia</em> (fellowship), to the breaking of bread and to prayer (Acts 2:42). <em>Koinōnia</em> means sharing something in common. Paul uses the same word for the Lord’s Supper: the cup is a ‘sharing’ in Christ’s blood, the bread a ‘sharing’ in his body, and ‘we being many are one bread, and one body’ (KJV) (1 Corinthians 10:16–17).",
        "That is why Paul is so angry at Corinth. Rich members ate first while poor members went hungry (1 Corinthians 11:20–22). He says they eat ‘not discerning the Lord’s body’ (11:29). Many scholars think ‘the body’ here includes the community itself. To shame the poor at the table is to miss Christ.",
        "One of the oldest Christian prayers, from the Didache, puts it beautifully: as the bread was scattered over the hills and gathered into one loaf, so let the church be gathered from the ends of the earth."
      ],
      refs: ["Acts 2:42–47", "1 Cor 10:16–17", "1 Cor 11:17–34", "Acts 4:32–35"]
    },
    {
      title: "How the early church lived it",
      body: [
        "The second-century <em>Letter to Diognetus</em> describes Christians as people who live in their own countries ‘simply as sojourners’, who ‘love all men, and are persecuted by all’. It sums up: what the soul is in the body, Christians are in the world. The church exists for the life of the world, not apart from it.",
        "Tertullian, around AD 197, describes how the church’s common fund was used: to support and bury the poor, to care for orphans and the elderly, the shipwrecked, and prisoners. He reports that outsiders said, ‘See how they love one another.’",
        "Ignatius of Antioch, around AD 110, judged false teachers partly by their lack of love: they had no care for the widow, the orphan, the oppressed, the hungry or the thirsty. For him, right belief and practical mercy belonged together. He also wrote that ‘wherever Jesus Christ is, there is the Catholic Church’. Christ’s presence makes the church, not the other way round."
      ],
      refs: ["Acts 6:1–7", "Jas 1:27", "Heb 13:1–3", "John 13:34–35"]
    },
    {
      title: "Counter-reading: ‘outside the church, no salvation’?",
      body: [
        "Many Christians have held a much stronger view of the church than the one presented here. Cyprian of Carthage in the third century said there is no salvation outside the church, and Catholic and Orthodox Christians see the church, with its sacraments, as the ark of salvation (compare 1 Peter 3:20–21). Many evangelicals stress church membership and discipline to protect the purity of a community of believers (1 Corinthians 5).",
        "These views take seriously something true: the New Testament never imagines a Christian life without the church. We are to keep ‘not forsaking our own assembling together’ (Hebrews 10:25).",
        "The relational view keeps the church necessary but changes its role. The church is not the gate that decides who God loves. It is the place where God’s love is known, celebrated and lived, and the body through which Christ reaches the world. Ignatius’s order matters: first ‘wherever Jesus Christ is’, then ‘there is the church’."
      ],
      refs: ["Heb 10:24–25", "1 Cor 5:1–5", "2 Cor 2:5–8", "1 Tim 2:5", "Matt 18:20"]
    },
    {
      title: "A priesthood with gifts for everyone",
      body: [
        "‘A royal priesthood’ (1 Peter 2:9) comes from God’s word to Israel at Sinai: ‘you shall be to me a kingdom of priests’ (Exodus 19:6). Revelation repeats it: Christ ‘made us to be a kingdom, priests to his God and Father’ (Revelation 1:6). Priesthood belongs to the whole people.",
        "Paul says ‘to each one is given the manifestation of the Spirit for the profit of all’ (1 Corinthians 12:7), and when the church gathers ‘each one of you has a psalm, has a teaching’ (14:26). Leaders exist to equip, not to replace, the rest (Ephesians 4:11–12).",
        "Martin Luther made this a rallying point in 1520, in his <em>Address to the Christian Nobility</em>, arguing that all baptised Christians belong to the ‘spiritual estate’. The relational gospel adds: every believer carries the Father’s reconciling heart into ordinary places, homes, workplaces and streets."
      ],
      refs: ["1 Pet 2:9", "Exod 19:6", "Rev 1:6", "1 Cor 12:4–27", "1 Cor 14:26", "Eph 4:11–16"]
    },
    {
      title: "Practical meaning: a family with open doors",
      body: [
        "Start at the table. Eat with other believers, and make sure someone poorer or lonelier is at your table too (Luke 14:12–14).",
        "Keep short accounts. Jesus says reconciliation with a brother or sister comes before your offering (Matthew 5:23–24). A church that practises forgiveness preaches the gospel without a sermon.",
        "Remember the forgotten: prisoners, widows, orphans, refugees (Hebrews 13:3; James 1:27). The early church was known for this. It can be again."
      ],
      refs: ["Luke 14:12–14", "Matt 5:23–24", "Heb 13:2–3", "Rom 12:13", "Jas 1:27"]
    }
  ],
  evidence: [
    { text: "Ekklēsia: ‘properly, a gathering of citizens called out from their homes into some public place; an assembly’; in the Septuagint ‘often equivalent to קָהָל, the assembly of the Israelites’.", source: "Thayer’s Greek Lexicon, via Bible Hub 1577", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/1577.htm" },
    { text: "‘The English word “church” comes from the Greek word kyriakos, “belonging to the Lord” (kyrios).’", source: "HELPS Word-studies, via Bible Hub 1577", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/1577.htm" },
    { text: "‘Even as this broken bread was scattered over the hills, and was gathered together and became one, so let Your Church be gathered together from the ends of the earth into Your kingdom’.", source: "Didache 9", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0714.htm" },
    { text: "‘They dwell in their own countries, but simply as sojourners … They love all men, and are persecuted by all.’ And: ‘what the soul is in the body, Christians are in the world.’", source: "Letter to Diognetus 5–6", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0101.htm" },
    { text: "The common fund is used ‘to support and bury poor people, to supply the wants of boys and girls destitute of means and parents, and of old persons confined now to the house’ … ‘See, they say, how they love one another’.", source: "Tertullian, Apology 39", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0301.htm" },
    { text: "Of false teachers: ‘They have no regard for love; no care for the widow, or the orphan, or the oppressed; of the bond, or of the free; of the hungry, or of the thirsty.’", source: "Ignatius, Letter to the Smyrnaeans 6", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0109.htm" },
    { text: "‘Wherever the bishop shall appear, there let the multitude [of the people] also be; even as, wherever Jesus Christ is, there is the Catholic Church.’", source: "Ignatius, Letter to the Smyrnaeans 8", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0109.htm" }
  ]
};

